# Scroll

Quick reference for `{{componentsPath}}/Scroll/Index.vue`, generated by
`ms-front-kit add composable useScroll`. This file belongs to the project; edit
it freely.

## Setup

Scroll is mounted once in the app shell, around the routed content:

```vue
<Scroll>
  <RouterView />  <!-- Nuxt: <NuxtLayout><NuxtPage /></NuxtLayout> -->
</Scroll>
```

| Prop | Values | Default | Description |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `mode` | `"auto"` \| `"virtual"` \| `"native"` | `"auto"` | `auto` uses virtual scroll without touch support and native scroll with it. |
| `scrollbar` | `boolean` | `true` | macOS-style overlay scrollbar in virtual mode. Native mode always keeps the browser's bar. |

- The Scroll root is `var(--vh, 100svh)` tall and is the only scroll container.
  The window never scrolls, so mobile toolbars stay put.
- Use `var(--vh)` instead of `100vh` for full-viewport sections.
- Keep fixed UI (header, menus, modals) outside Scroll.
- The content element gets `data-scroll-mode="virtual|native"` for styling, for
  example `[data-scroll-mode="virtual"] .hero { ... }`.

## Modes

- **virtual**: wheel, keyboard, and touch input are eased, and the marked
  elements are moved with transforms.
- **native**: the browser scrolls the root, and Scroll only reads its position.
  Attributes marked "virtual only" below have no effect in this mode, so
  layouts must also work without them.

## Data attributes you add

Boolean attributes need no value: `<section data-scroll>`. Numbers are px.

| Attribute | Mode | Value | What it does |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `data-scroll` | virtual | — | Moves the element with the scroll. Mark each large top-level section. |
| `data-scroll-continuous` | virtual | — | Use with `data-scroll`. The element keeps moving while off-screen instead of being parked (for content that overflows its box or is tracked elsewhere). |
| `data-scroll-sticky` | virtual | — | Sticks inside its parent. The offset is the CSS `top`. |
| `data-scroll-sticky-bottom` | virtual | px | Space to keep between the sticky element and the bottom of its parent. Default `0`. |
| `data-scroll-fixed-from` | virtual | px | Scroll position where the element stops moving. Needs `data-scroll-fixed-to`. |
| `data-scroll-fixed-to` | virtual | px | Scroll position where the element starts moving again. |
| `data-scroll-set-progress` | both | — | Writes `data-scroll-progress` and `data-scroll-current` (see below). |
| `data-scroll-set-position` | both | — | Writes `data-position-top/-left/-right/-bottom` when measured. |
| `data-scroll-target-offset` | both | px | Distance kept above the element when `scrollTo` targets it, for example a fixed header's height. |
| `data-tab-fixed` | virtual | — | Keyboard focus on this element does not scroll it into view. |
| `data-scroll-lock-allow` | both | — | While scrolling is disabled, this container (modal, menu) keeps scrolling internally without moving the page. |

### Rules for virtual mode

- If **no** element has `data-scroll`, the whole content moves as one block, and
  `position: fixed` descendants then move with it.
- If **any** element has `data-scroll`, only marked elements move: mark every
  top-level section, or unmarked content stays in place.
- Do not nest `data-scroll`, `data-scroll-sticky`, or `data-scroll-fixed-*`
  elements inside each other. A moving element inside a moving section moves
  twice. Sticky elements are the exception: they belong inside a marked section,
  as below.
- Mark a few large sections rather than many small elements, because each
  moving element is a compositor layer. Off-screen sections skip writes.

### Sticky in both modes

Write the CSS for native mode and add the attribute for virtual mode:

```vue
<section data-scroll class="features">
  <aside data-scroll-sticky data-scroll-sticky-bottom="40" class="features__nav">...</aside>
  <div class="features__list">...</div>
</section>
```

```scss
.features__nav {
  position: sticky; // Native mode. In virtual mode, the transform takes over.
  top: 80px;        // Read as the sticky offset in virtual mode too.
}
```

### Pinning a block between two positions (virtual)

```vue
<div data-scroll-fixed-from="0" data-scroll-fixed-to="1200" class="intro">...</div>
```

The element moves normally until a scroll position of `0`, stays fixed until
`1200`, and then scrolls away. Place it outside any `data-scroll` section.

## Data attributes Scroll writes

| Attribute | On | Value |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `data-scroll-progress` | `data-scroll-set-progress` | `0.00`–`1.00`. It is `0` when the element's top reaches the bottom of the viewport and `1` when its bottom leaves the top. |
| `data-scroll-current` | `data-scroll-set-progress` | px scrolled since the element entered the viewport (`0` before). |
| `data-position-top`, `data-position-bottom` | `data-scroll-set-position` | Content-relative px, independent of the current scroll. |
| `data-position-left`, `data-position-right` | `data-scroll-set-position` | Viewport px. |
| `data-scroll-sticky-state` | `data-scroll-sticky` | `"true"` while stuck, otherwise `"false"`. Useful for styling. |
| `data-scroll-sticky-limit-top`, `data-scroll-sticky-limit-bottom` | `data-scroll-sticky` | Scroll position where sticking starts, and how far the element can travel while stuck. |
| `data-scroll-mode` | content element | `"virtual"` or `"native"`. |
| `data-scroll-find` | Scroll root | Present while find in page scrolls the root natively (virtual mode). |

```scss
.features__nav[data-scroll-sticky-state="true"] {
  box-shadow: 0 1px 0 rgb(0 0 0 / 10%);
}
```

## Store

`{{storePath}}/useScrollStore.ts` (Pinia). Import it relatively or through your
alias. Never use `window.scrollY`, `window.scrollTo`, window scroll listeners,
or the router's `scrollBehavior`.

```ts
const scroll = useScrollStore();

scroll.scrollTo("#about");                          // id (with or without #), element, or px
scroll.scrollTo(0, { immediate: true });            // jump without easing
scroll.scrollTo(element, { align: "center" });      // "start" (default) or "center"
scroll.disable();                                   // lock scrolling (modals); disable(false) unlocks
scroll.update();                                    // re-measure after layout changes
scroll.reset();                                     // back to the top and re-measure
scroll.setMode("native");                           // usually set through <Scroll mode>

const stop = scroll.addRenderCallback(data => {
  // Per-frame work: animations, WebGL, DOM writes.
  title.style.opacity = String(1 - data.current / 400);
});
stop();                                             // or scroll.removeRenderCallback(callback)
```

| State | Type | Description |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `mode` | `"auto" \| "virtual" \| "native"` | Requested mode. |
| `activeMode` | `"virtual" \| "native" \| undefined` | Mode in use (`auto` resolved). |
| `isVirtual` | `boolean` | `activeMode === "virtual"`. |
| `disabled` | `boolean` | Scroll locked. |
| `data` | `Data` | Latest values, replaced on every animated frame. |

`Data`, received by render callbacks:

| Field | Description |
| --- | --- |
| `current` | Rendered scroll position in px (eased). |
| `currentVertical` | `current` minus the distance travelled by stuck sticky elements. |
| `speed` | Remaining distance to the target in px (`0` at rest). |
| `direction` | `"up"` or `"down"`. |
| `bounding` | Maximum scroll position (content height minus viewport). |
| `inTarget` | `true` once the eased position has reached the target. |

Read `data` inside render callbacks, not through watchers or template bindings,
because it changes on every frame. Callbacks run before reactive consumers.

## Behavior to know

- **Resizing**: content size changes are measured automatically (ResizeObserver).
  Call `update()` after changes it cannot see, such as transforms or late class
  toggles.
- **Routes**: path changes reset to the top. With page transitions, call
  `reset()` or `update()` once the new page has entered.
- **Anchors**: route hashes (`/page#about`) scroll to the matching id in both
  modes, including on first load.
- **Keyboard (virtual)**: arrows move 100px; Page Up/Down and Space move 90% of
  the viewport (Shift+Space goes up); Home and End go to the limits. These keys
  are ignored while typing in a field.
- **Focus (virtual)**: elements focused with the keyboard that are out of view
  are centered, unless they have `data-tab-fixed`.
- **Reduced motion**: `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` disables easing.
- **Find in page (virtual)**: Cmd/Ctrl+F works.
  - When the find bar opens or takes focus, Scroll hands the current position
    to a native scroll of the root and keeps every section in place, so the
    browser can reach any match.
  - It looks the same as virtual mode: sticky, fixed-from/to, and the scrollbar
    keep working.
  - The first wheel, key, or touch on the page returns to virtual mode from
    wherever the search left off.
  - Limitations: while the find bar is in use, scrolling is native, without
    easing. Searching while a scroll animation is still running only takes
    effect once the animation ends. While scrolling is disabled (modals), find
    in page does not move the page.

## Scrollbar (virtual mode)

The scrollbar behaves like the macOS overlay bar:

- It appears while scrolling and fades after 1s.
- It widens while hovered or held.
- You can drag the thumb.
- Clicking the track pages toward the pointer, and holding repeats.
- Option/Alt-click jumps to the clicked spot.
- The wheel works over it.

Over dark backgrounds, the thumb turns light automatically. Restyle it in
Index.vue:

```scss
.scroll__bar {
  --scroll-thumb: rgb(0 0 0 / 50%);
  --scroll-thumb-edge: rgb(255 255 255 / 50%);
  --scroll-track: rgb(250 250 250 / 90%);
  --scroll-track-border: rgb(0 0 0 / 8%);
}
```

Available state attributes on `.scroll__bar`:

- `data-visible`
- `data-active` (held)
- `data-background="dark|light"`
- `data-overflow="true|false"`

Turn it off with `<Scroll :scrollbar="false">`.
